(688,13,-17,0,'Dirt Road','  This road is dry and barren, with great ruts where wagons have traveled.\nThe road here is fairly level, but to the east, a great range of high\nmountains loom on the horizon.  To the northwest, a great, walled city\nsits atop a plateau.\n',11,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(689,7,-20,0,'Wet Road','  This dirt road is cold and damp.  There are groups of insects flying about.\nYou see a swamp to the west.\n',11,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(690,7,-21,0,'Wet Road','  This dirt road is cold and damp.  There are groups of insects flying about.\nYou see a swamp to the west.\n',11,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(691,7,-22,0,'Old Bridge','  You are on a sloppy bridge over the River Kranor.  The road is still covered\nwith mud and there are footprints on the bridge.  Also, a large puddle of water\nis here.\n',11,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(692,7,-23,0,'Muddy Road','  This road looks like it is supposed to be dirt, but with the large amount of\nstanding water, it is quite muddy.  There are a few puddles of dirty water and\na lot of footprints in the mud.\n',11,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(693,7,-24,0,'Muddy Road','  This road looks like it is supposed to be dirt, but with the large amount of\nstanding water, it is quite muddy.  There are huge ruts in the mud where it\nlooks like a wagon got stuck!\n',11,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(694,6,-25,0,'Muddy Road','  This road looks like it is supposed to be dirt, but with the large amount of\nstanding water, it is quite muddy.\n',11,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(695,5,-26,0,'Poor Road','  This road is in pretty poor shape.  It seems as if the combination of water\nand usage have led to the premature decay of this road.\n',11,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(696,4,-27,0,'Poor Road','  This road is in pretty poor shape.  It seems as if the combination of water\nand usage have led to the premature decay of this road.\n',11,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(697,3,-28,0,'Poor Road','  This road is in pretty poor shape.  It seems as if the combination of water\nand usage have led to the premature decay of this road.  You see a jungle in\nthe distance to the south.\n',11,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(698,2,-29,0,'Poor Road','  This road is in pretty poor shape.  It seems as if the combination of water\nand usage have led to the premature decay of this road.  To the south you see a\nlarge jungle.  A damp looking cave is just barely visible through the\nvegetation to the southeast.\n',11,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(699,1,-30,0,'Bend in the Road','  This bend in the road leads to the north into a large swamp.  To the east is\na poorly maintained road.  To the south, you see the start of a large jungle.\nA small clearing is southwest.\n',11,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(700,-19,-17,0,'Stone Road','This road looks extremely old, but is in pretty good shape.  The road is made\nof stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in between the stones where\nmortar once existed.  To the north, you see the entrance to a stable.\n',-1,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(701,-20,-18,0,'Stone Road','This road looks extremely old, but is in pretty good shape.  The road is made\nof stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in between the stones where\nmortar once existed.\n',-1,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(702,-21,-19,0,'Stone Road','  This road looks extremely old, but is in pretty good shape.  The road is made\nof stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in between the stones where\nmortar once existed.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(703,-22,-20,0,'Stone Road','  This road looks extremely old, but is in pretty good shape.  The road is made\nof stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in between the stones where\nmortar once existed.  To the west you see the start of a large mountain range.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(704,-23,-20,0,'Stone Road','  This road looks extremely old, but is in pretty good shape.  The road is made\nof stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in between the stones where\nmortar once existed.  This road continues west through a mountain pass and east\ninto a clearing.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(705,-24,-20,0,'Road through Foothills','  The road is made of stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in\nbetween the stones where mortar once existed.  The road continues west through\nthe foothills and east towards a clearing.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(706,-25,-20,0,'Road through Foothills','  The road is made of stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in\nbetween the stones where mortar once existed.  The road continues west through\nthe foothills and east towards a clearing.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(707,-26,-20,0,'Road through Foothills','  The road is made of stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in\nbetween the stones where mortar once existed.  The road forms into a mountain\npass to the west and continues through the foothills to the east.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(708,-27,-20,0,'Mountain Pass','  The road is made of stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in\nbetween the stones where mortar once existed.  The road is narrow but clear,\nand continues through the pass to the west.  The road widens to the east as it\nenters the foothills.\n',12,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(709,-28,-20,0,'Mountain Pass','  The road is made of stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in\nbetween the stones where mortar once existed.  The road is narrow but clear,\nand continues through the pass to the east and west.\n',12,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(710,0,0,0,'Mountain Pass','The road was once made of stones tightly fitted together, but where there was\nonce smooth stone and mortar, there are swaths of black scorch marks and\nenormous pockmarks, making the road rough and rocky.  Piles of rough black rock\nlike lava have been swept to each side of the trail, which continues through the\npass to the east and west.\n',0,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(711,-30,-20,0,'Mountain Pass','  The road is made of stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in\nbetween the stones where mortar once existed.  The road is narrow but clear,\nand continues through the pass to the east and west.  You see a narrow trail\nleading southward.\n',12,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(712,0,0,0,'Mountain Pass','  The road is made of stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in\nbetween the stones where mortar once existed.  The road is narrow but clear,\nand continues through the pass to the east and west. The road is scarred with scorched pock marks.\n',0,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(713,-32,-20,0,'Mountain Pass','  The road is made of stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in\nbetween the stones where mortar once existed.  The road is narrow but clear,\nand continues through the pass to the east and west.\n',12,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(714,-33,-20,0,'Mountain Pass','  The road is made of stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in\nbetween the stones where mortar once existed.  The road is narrow but clear,\nand continues through the pass to the east.  The road widens to the west as it\nenters the foothills. The slightly odd formation of a rock wall catches your\nattention.\n',12,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(715,-34,-20,0,'Road through Foothills','  The road is made of stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in\nbetween the stones where mortar once existed.  The road continues east through\nthe foothills and west towards a clearing.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(716,-35,-20,0,'Road through Foothills','  The road is made of stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in\nbetween the stones where mortar once existed.  The road continues east through\nthe foothills and west towards a clearing.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(717,-30,-21,0,'Trail through the Mountains','  This narrow trail rises sharply upward from the mountain pass.  The trail is\nworn into the dirt, and is covered by heavy vegetation.\n',12,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(718,-31,-22,0,'Trail through the Mountains','  This narrow trail rises sharply upward from the mountain pass.  The trail is\nworn into the dirt, and is covered by heavy vegetation.\n',12,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(719,-32,-23,0,'Trail through the Mountains','  The trail if clear and wide here.  You see a trail to the northeast and a\nlarge clearing to the southwest.\n',12,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(720,-33,-24,0,'Large Clearing','  This clearing is different from anything else you have seen around this area.\nThe ground is level smooth.  You see the charred remains of a small campfire,\nand several boot prints on the ground.\n',12,131072,23,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(721,-32,-25,0,'Trail through the Mountains','  The brush here is not as dense as other parts in this area, but gets more\ndense to the south.  You see a large clearing to the northwest.\n',12,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(722,-32,-26,0,'Trail through the Mountains','  The trail continues its rocky, uneven pattern here.  You see the forest clear\nto the north and grow very dense to the southeast.\n',12,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(723,-31,-27,0,'Trail through the Mountains','  The trail bends here from the northwest to the southwest.  The vegetation is\ndeep!  It is hard to tell if it is day or night!\n',12,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(724,-32,-28,0,'Trail through the Mountains','  There is a clearing in the trees overhead here.  In the distance to the\nsoutheast, you see the top of a small volcano.\n',12,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(725,-31,-29,0,'Trail through the Mountains','  The brush here becomes extremely dense.  You can see a small trail cut\nthrough the vegetation to the southeast and a better trail to the northwest.\n',12,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(726,-30,-30,0,'Trail through the Mountains','  The brush here continues to be very dense.  You can see the trail widening to\nthe northwest, and narrow to the south.\n',12,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(727,-30,-31,0,'The Mountains','  The trail seems to be absorbed into the thick brush here.  You can make a\nfaint trail to the east and to the north.  To the south an overgrown\ntrail winds it way through the vegetation.  It seems the trail to the east \nleads into some sort of dense vegetation.\n',12,131072,24,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(728,-36,-20,0,'Road through Foothills','  The road is made of stones, tightly fitted together.  There is dirt in\nbetween the stones where mortar once existed.  The road continues east through\nthe foothills and west towards a clearing.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(729,-37,-20,0,'A Road through the Prairie','  You are traveling along a clear road.  The road travels west to east.  You\nsee a mountain pass to the east and a road through a clearing to the west.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(730,-38,-20,0,'A Road through the Prairie','  You are traveling along a clear road.  The road travels west to east.  You\nsee a mountain pass to the east and a road through a clearing to the west.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(731,-39,-20,0,'A Road through the Prairie','  You are traveling along a clear road.  The road travels west to east.  You\nsee mountains far off to the east and west.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(732,-40,-21,0,'A Road through the Prairie','  You are traveling along a clear road.  The road travels west to east.  You\nsee mountains far off to the east and west.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(733,-41,-22,0,'A Road through the Prairie','  You are traveling along a clear road.  The road travels west to east.  You\nsee mountains far off to the east and much closer ones to the west.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(734,-42,-23,0,'A Road through the Prairie','  You are traveling along a clear road.  The road travels west to east.  A\nrange of tall hills lies to the west.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(735,-43,-24,0,'A Road through the Prairie','  You are on a long, narrow road which passes through a prairie.  A range\nof tall hills lies to the east.  The grasses are long and dry, with the\nwind from the north.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(736,-44,-25,0,'A Road through the Prairie','  You are on a long, narrow road which passes through the heart of a large\nprairie.  The grasses are long and dry, with the wind from the north.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(737,-45,-26,0,'A Road through the Prairie','  You are on a long, narrow road which passes through the heart of a large\nprairie.  The grasses are long and dry, with the wind from the north.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(738,-46,-27,0,'A Road through the Prairie','  You are on a long, narrow road which passes through the heart of a large\nprairie.  The grasses are long and dry, with the wind from the north.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(739,-47,-28,0,'A Road through the Prairie','  You are on a long, narrow road which passes through the heart of a large\nprairie.  The grasses are long and dry, with the wind from the north.  You see\na large city off to the west.\n',12,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(743,-61,-35,0,'South Coastal Highway','  This broad road runs south from Brightmoon along the shore of the\nAgorian Sea.  Looking westward, you see the rocky shoreline, beyond\nwhich the sparkling blue waters stretch to the horizon.  Looking east,\nyou see a rolling grassland.  A short way to the north, you see the walls\nof the city of Brightmoon.  The road continues to the north and south.\n',18,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(744,-61,-36,0,'South Coastal Highway','  This broad road runs south from Brightmoon along the shore of the\nAgorian Sea.  Looking westward, you see the rocky shoreline, beyond\nwhich the sparkling blue waters stretch to the horizon.  Looking east,\nyou see a rolling grassland.  A little way to the north, you see the\nwalls of the city of Brightmoon.  The road continues to the north and\nsouth.\n',18,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(745,-61,-37,0,'South Coastal Highway','  This broad road runs south from Brightmoon along the shore of the\nAgorian Sea.  Looking westward, you see the rocky shoreline, beyond\nwhich the sparkling blue waters stretch to the horizon.  Looking east,\nyou see a rolling grassland.  A way to the north, you see the walls of\nthe city of Brightmoon.  The road continues to the north and south.\n',18,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(746,-61,-38,0,'South Coastal Highway','  This broad road runs south from Brightmoon along the shore of the\nAgorian Sea.  Looking westward, you see the rocky shoreline, beyond\nwhich the sparkling blue waters stretch to the horizon.  Looking east,\nyou see a rolling grassland.  A fair way to the north, you see the\nwalls of the city of Brightmoon.  The road continues to the north and\nsouth.\n',18,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(747,-61,-39,0,'South Coastal Highway','  This broad road runs south from Brightmoon along the shore of the\nAgorian Sea.  Looking westward, you see the rocky shoreline, beyond\nwhich the sparkling blue waters stretch to the horizon.  Looking east,\nyou see a rolling grassland.  Far to the north, you see the walls of\nthe city of Brightmoon.  To the south, a bridge crosses a ravine.  The\nroad continues to the north and south.\n',18,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(748,-61,-40,0,'Highway Bridge Over the Silverstone River','  A wooden bridge carries the Coastal Highway over a ravine through which\nthe Silverstone River flows westward to the Agorian Sea.  The water is at\nleast 60 feet below you.  Looking westward, you can see the broad, flat\ndelta where the river and sea meet.\n',18,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(749,-61,-41,0,'South Coastal Highway','This broad road runs south from Brightmoon along the shore of the\nAgorian Sea.  Looking westward, you see the rocky shoreline, beyond\nwhich the sparkling blue waters stretch to the horizon.  Looking east,\nyou see an arid plain.  Just to the north, a bridge crosses a ravine.\nThe road continues to the north and south.\n',-1,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(750,-5,1,0,'Monk Entry','  This room has mud on the floor, and is used to provide the monks with easy\naccess to the church.  The monks of Galek are a quiet and mysterious lot.\nThey are rarely seen here for various reasons they keep to themselves;\nyet it is not uncommon to see the occasional monk walking quietly northwards\ndown the hall to spend time with his master and teacher or simply to take a\nshort nap to recharge themselves when meditation fails to do so.  A hallway\nto the east leads into the main section of the church and also to the West\nSteeple Tower.  There is a well used door to your west.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(751,-4,1,0,'West Steeple Tower','  A stone staircase leads upstairs to the Colonnade balconies where the\ncommonfolk tend to congregate to watch the services performed by the Head\nPriest.  Any royal ceremonies are watched by the masses from this vantage\npoint as well, as the nave and pews on the first floor are occupied by either\nRoyalty or esteemed guests.  The staircase will also bring you to the steeple\ntower itself where you can view our illustrious city and surrounding areas.\nThe entrance to the church is to the east and a passage to the monks quarters\nis to the west.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(752,-3,1,0,'Church Entry','  This is the main entrance to the church.  Barrel vaulted ceilings make\nthe entrance impressive to behold and the altar directly ahead seems remote\nand distant with the soaring heights of the architecture.  This church was\nconstructed by the Brotherhood of Galek with the permission of the King of\nGrimhaven; the Brotherhood uses it as a place of worship and as a monument\nand centerpiece to disseminate the teachings of Galek.  Hallways leading\nto the steeple towers are to your east and west.  You see a large door on the\nsouth wall leading back to West King\'s Street.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(753,-2,1,0,'East Steeple Tower','  A stone staircase leads upstairs to the Colonnade balconies where the\ncommonfolk tend to congregate to watch the services performed by the Head\nPriest.  Any royal ceremonies are watched by the masses from this vantage\npoint as well, as the nave and pews on the first floor are occupied by either\nRoyalty or esteemed guests.  The staircase will also bring you to the steeple\ntower itself where you can view our illustrious city and surrounding areas.\nThe entrance to the church is to the west and a small orphanage is to your\neast.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(754,-1,1,0,'Orphanage','  This is the home for the wayward boys and girls of this town.  It is\nwarm and inviting so as to make the children feel safe and secure.  To\nyour west is the staircase to the East Steeple Tower and to the north\nis a larger area of the orphanage.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(755,-5,2,0,'Monk\'s Quarters','  The hallway is extremely narrow and nondescript.  The hall widens to\nthe south and continues north.  There is little furniture here, just a few\nsleeping mats.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(756,-4,2,0,'Aisle','  This long and narrow aisle is used mostly to lead people to and from\nthe transept in the north where the congregation can sit and pray.\nThe floors are polished smooth due to all the use it has seen.  To your\neast is the nave.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(757,-3,2,0,'Nave','  This long passage is filled with pews for the congregation to sit and\npray.  Vaulted ceilings soar above you into the gloomy heights of the church.\nYou can see some people above looking down from the colonnade balconies.  The\nnave continues north and to your east and west are aisles in which to access\nthe transept.  The magnificent entrance to the church lies to your south.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,24,'',NULL),
(758,-2,2,0,'Long Aisle','  This long and narrow aisle is used mostly to lead people to and from\nthe transept in the north where the congregation can sit and pray.\nThe floors are polished smooth due to all the use it has seen.  To your\nwest is the nave.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,24,'',NULL),
(759,-1,2,0,'Orphanage Entrance','  This is the main entrance of the orphanage.  The orphanage is run by\nmissionaries of The Brotherhood of Galek who built this church.  The\nchildren are well cared for and well regarded by most of the city populace.\nThere is a door out to main street to the east.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(760,-5,3,0,'Monk\'s Quarters','  The hallway is extremely narrow and nondescript.  You hear some chanting\nto the north.  The hall continues north to the monks study area and south\nto the entryway.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(761,-4,3,0,'West Transept','  You are in the west side of the transept.  From here you can sit\nand pray in comfort and silence.  The transept is used by most to use\nfor prayer and solitude when the church is not in the process of giving\na service to the congregation.  To your east is the nave and its\nimpressive vaulted ceilings and to the south is a long narrow aisle\nwhich will eventually bring you back to the entrance of the church.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,24,'',NULL),
(762,-3,3,0,'Nave & Transcept','  This part of the nave intersects with the transept completing the cross\nlike floor plan of the cruciform.  Vaulted ceilings soar above you into the\ngloomy heights of the church.  Pews fill this area and are used by the masses\non days when services are being performed for the populace as a whole.  You\ncan see some people from above looking down from the colonnade balconies.  A\nmagnificent altar is to your north.  The transept continues to your east and\nwest.  The long nave continues south onwards to the grand entrance of the\nchurch.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(763,-2,3,0,'East Transept','  You are in the east side of the transept.  From here you can sit\nand pray in comfort and silence.  The transept is used by most to use\nfor prayer and solitude when the church is not in the process of giving\na service to the congregation.  To your west is the nave and its\nimpressive vaulted ceilings and to the south is a long narrow aisle\nwhich will eventually bring you back to the entrance of the church.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,24,'',NULL),
(764,-1,3,0,'Orphanage','  This is the home for the wayward boys and girls of this town.  It is\nwarm and inviting so as to make the children feel safe and secure.  Most\nof the administrative tasks of running the orphanage start and end here\nbut the toys and dolls near the play room are starting to spill out into\nthis area as well.  The staff that care for the boys and girls try to\nrestrict the mess to the play room but are rarely, if ever, successful.\nFortunately for the children, the missionaries here are very kind.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(765,-5,4,0,'Monk\'s Study & Meditation Room','  Monk\'s come here from all over the land to find a little solitude and a\nplace to catch up on their studies.  To the north is the Grandmaster Monk\'s\nLair and to the south is the Monk\'s Quarters.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(766,-4,4,0,'Confession','  Small booths adorn this room where the sinful may confess to their\nclergyman and power above to hopefully absolve them of their sins.\nVelvet carpet makes the floor warm and comfortable and candles sit upon\na few tables casting light on this otherwise dim room.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(767,-3,4,0,'Altar','  There are few places in The World that can match the spectacular\nbeauty and craftsmanship that was focused on this work of art.  The\naltar itself is made of solid gold as a sign of worship and respect\nto the all powerful.  From here the leader of this congregation\ncan conduct his services in a fashion that would make that which\nhe serves proud.  There is a door to the north that leads to the\napse.  To the south is the nave, and a long walk to the entrance\nof the church.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(768,-2,4,0,'Choir','  As in traditional churches, the choir is set in the eastern end of this\none as well.  Beautiful and wondrous music has been performed here for\nyears on end.  There are no choir members here at the moment, but perhaps\nif you stick around you will get to hear that which has led many in\nthis city to cry and hug their loved ones in joy and sadness.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(769,-1,4,0,'Play Room','  This place is just filled with toys and dolls of everyconceivable type.\nIt is pure madness in this room and it\'s hard not to smile even on your\nworst days.  Usually, there are several little children playing and\nlaughing.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(770,-5,5,0,'Grandmaster Monk\'s Lair','  This is where most students who plan on reaching tremendous insight into\nthemselves and others come for intensive study and training by the Master\nhimself.  The room is sparse, and the walls and ceiling are soot covered by\nthe smoke cast from a single torch that illuminates the room.  The ceiling\nlooks to be smeared a little in one place.  To the south you hear some\nchanting.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(771,-4,5,0,'Priest\'s Study','  This office is bright and pleasant.  A large desk sits against the west wall\nand books and papers are scattered all over it.  A pointed lancet is on the\nnorth wall to let sunlight in which leads to a really warm and comfortable\nplace to learn or talk to a priest.\n',13,131117,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(772,-3,5,0,'Apse','  This is a semi circular room at the back of the cathedral.  There\nare small doors to your east and west and a trapdoor above leading\nto a section of the North Steeple Tower.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(773,-2,5,0,'<C>Room of Remembrance<1>','  This room is used by those who care to remember those they have lost\nand loved.  A small area is set aside for people to leave something\nspecial that will remind others of whom the loved one was.  There\nseems to be a space near the east wall open if you care to leave\nanything.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(774,-1,5,0,'Storage','  This is the secret room the church uses to store personal effects\nleft in the room of remembrance when it begins to crowd up the room.\nA clear spot is on the west wall.  Should you be here???\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,4,'',NULL),
(775,-3,5,1,'A Room In The North Steeple Tower','  Inside this thatched room are simple peasant quarters.  A loom stands by\na small window, oil lamps hang above the low rafters.  A wooden bed is seen\nflush against the south wall by an oval table that harbors a set of silver\ncandalabrae smoldering with a strong scent of incense.  A trapdoor is set\nin the floor.\n',13,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(800,-10,-1,0,'Barracks: Garrison of Chaos','  This is the barracks for the Grimhaven Garrison.  The outer walls on the\nnorth and west walls are of fortified stonework, while the other two inner\nwalls are of solid timber.  An open doorway is at the northern end of the\neast wall leading out to a hallway.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(801,-9,-1,0,'Barracks: Garrison of Chaos','  This is the barracks for the Grimhaven Garrison.  The outer walls on the\nnorth and west walls are of fortified stonework, while the other two inner\nwalls are of solid timber.  An open doorway is at the northern end of the\neast wall leading out to a hallway.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(802,-8,-1,0,'Entryway into the Garrison of Chaos','The Cult of Chaos maintains a small garrison unit in Grimhaven to bolster\nthe local cityguard militia and to help spread the cult teachings.  The outer\nwalls are made of stone and mortar, while the inner walls are of solid timber.\nWest leads an open doorway into the barracks and east is a simple doorway.\nNorth a fortified door leads through the outer stone wall of the garrison while\na hallway continues southwards.\n',0,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(803,-7,-1,0,'Armory: Garrison of Chaos','  A small armory serves the needs of the local garrison.  It seems poorly\nstocked with armament - perhaps that is a good sign as it means the Cult has\nno military objectives in the region.  A stout door leads west.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(804,-10,-2,0,'Barracks: Garrison of Chaos','  This is the barracks for the Grimhaven Garrison.  The outer walls on the\nnorth and west walls are of fortified stonework, while the other two inner\nwalls are of solid timber.  An open doorway is at the northern end of the\neast wall leading out to a hallway.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(805,-9,-2,0,'Barracks: Garrison of Chaos','  This is the barracks for the Grimhaven Garrison.  The outer walls on the\nnorth and west walls are of fortified stonework, while the other two inner\nwalls are of solid timber.  An open doorway is at the northern end of the\neast wall leading out to a hallway.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(806,-8,-2,0,'Garrison of Chaos','  A simple hallway stretches north and south down the center of the garrison.\nTo the west is a simple timber wall while a door in the east wall leads into\nthe dining hall.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(807,-7,-2,0,'Dining Hall: Garrison of Chaos','  A small dining hall serves the needs of the Chaos Garrison.  The timber\nfloor seems covered in a thin layer of greasy oil from years of spillages\nand other accidents.  A door on the west wall leads out to a hallway and\nanother door in the south wall heads into the kitchen.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(808,-10,-3,0,'Training Ground: Garrison of Chaos','  This is a small plot of flat ground within both the walls of Grimhaven and\nwithin the stone walls of the garrison.  It is open air and a fortified door\nin the northeast corner leads back into the garrison.\n',2,131073,23,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(809,-9,-3,0,'Training Ground: Garrison of Chaos','  This is a small plot of flat ground within both the walls of Grimhaven and\nwithin the stone walls of the garrison.  It is open air and a fortified door\nin the northeast corner leads back into the garrison.\n',2,131073,23,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(810,-8,-3,0,'Garrison of Chaos','  A simple hallway stretches north and south down the center of the garrison.\nTo the east is a simple timber wall while a fortified door in the west wall\nleads out to a training ground.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(811,-7,-3,0,'Dining Hall: Garrison of Chaos','  A small dining hall serves the needs of the Chaos Garrison.  The timber floor\nseems covered in a thin layer of greasy oil from years of spillages and other\naccidents.  A door in the northwest corner leads out to a hallway and another\nswinging door in the south wall heads into the kitchen.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(812,-10,-4,0,'Training Ground: Garrison of Chaos','  This is a small plot of flat ground within both the walls of Grimhaven and\nwithin the stone walls of the garrison.  It is open air and a fortified door\nin the northeast corner leads back into the garrison.\n',2,131073,23,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(813,-9,-4,0,'Training Ground: Garrison of Chaos','  This is a small plot of flat ground within both the walls of Grimhaven and\nwithin the stone walls of the garrison.  It is open air and a fortified door\nin the northeast corner leads back into the garrison.\n',2,131073,23,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(814,-8,-4,0,'Garrison of Chaos','  A simple hallway stretches north and south down the center of the garrison.\nTo the west is a solid stonework wall while a door in the east wall leads into\nthe kitchen.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(815,-7,-4,0,'Kitchen: Garrison of Chaos','  A simple kitchen serves the local garrison of the local Cult Garrison.\nThe east exterior wall is made of stone while elsewhere the walls are of solid\ntimber construction.  North a swinging door leads out to the dining area and\nwest a door heads out to the hallway.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(816,-9,-5,0,'Training Ground: Garrison of Chaos','  This is a small plot of flat ground within both the walls of Grimhaven and\nwithin the stone walls of the garrison.  It is open air and a fortified door\nin the northeast corner leads back into the garrison.\n',2,131073,23,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(817,-8,-5,0,'Garrison of Chaos','  A simple hallway stretches north and east down the center of the garrison.\nTo the west is a solid stonework wall while a door in the southwest wall leads\noutside to Perimeter Road.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(818,-7,-5,0,'Back Hallway: Garrison of Chaos','  A hallway stretches westward away from this back entrance into the Cult\'s\nGarrison.  A fortified doorway leads out through the eastern stonework wall to\nCommonwealth Lane, and another door leads south into the Commandant\'s\nQuarters.  Set into the floor is a trapdoor so expertly crafted as to almost\ngo unnoticed.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(819,-8,-6,0,'Commandant\'s Quarters: Garrison of Chaos','  Part of a large oddly shaped room in the southern corner of the garrison\nserves as both the office and living quarters for the garrison commandant.\nThe exterior walls are of stout stonework construction.  You see traces of\na puritan disposition in the lack of any decorative objects.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(820,-7,-6,0,'Commandant\'s Quarters: Garrison of Chaos','  Part of a large oddly shaped room in the southern corner of the garrison\nserves as both the office and living quarters for the garrison commandant.\nThe exterior walls are of stout stonework construction.  You see a puritan\ndisposition in the lack of any decorative objects.  There is a door to the\nnorth leading to a back hallway.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(821,-7,-7,0,'Commandant\'s Quarters: Garrison of Chaos','  Part of a large oddly shaped room in the southern corner of the garrison\nserves as both the office and living quarters for the garrison commandant.\nThe exterior walls are of stout stonework construction.  You see traces of\na puritan disposition in the lack of any decorative objects.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(850,1,-12,0,'Inside a Watchtower','  You are standing inside one of the city watchtowers.  The room smells of\nsweat and mildew.  You see a small door to the west and a stairway going up.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(851,1,-12,1,'Inside a Watchtower','  You are standing inside one of the city watchtowers.  You see a small arch to\nthe east leading along the top of the city wall.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(852,1,-12,2,'Atop a Watchtower','  You are standing on top of one of the city watchtowers.  You see another\nwatchtower to the west.  The view from here allows you to see the entire city\nand the vast expanse to the south.\n',2,131081,32,0,0,0,0,0,0,100,0,'',NULL),
(853,2,-12,1,'On the City Wall','  You are standing on top of the large city wall.  The wall encircles the\nentire city.  The walkway is about 8 feet across and about 30 feet high.  The\nwalkway is level and the stones are stuck together with a light-gray mortar.\n',2,131073,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
