(1135,29,42,-2,'A Trail Through Some Rocky Hills','  Rough terrain lies all about you as you make your way along what is\nlittle more than a gamepath.  The rock strewn hillsides on either side\nforce to you to stay to the trail if you wish to avoid spraining an ankle.\nScraggly brush grows in the gaps between the rocks, providing a hiding\nplace for who knows what type of wildlife.  An occasional pine or spruce\ntree grows atop the windswept hills.\n',130,131072,26,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1136,30,43,-2,'A Trail Through Some Rocky Hills','Rough terrain lies all about you as you make your way along what is\nlittle more than a gamepath.  The rock strewn hillsides on either side\nforce to you to stay to the trail if you wish to avoid spraining an ankle.\nScraggly brush grows in the gaps between the rocks, providing a hiding\nplace for who knows what type of wildlife.  An occasional pine or spruce\ntree grows atop the windswept hills.  A heavy thicket lies to the west.\n',-1,131072,26,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1150,-30,-32,0,'Overgrown Path','The path is badly overgrown, looking like no living being has treaded it\nin ages.  To the north, there are some hills, while off to the southeast\nyou see a huge volcano.  There seems to be a dense forest to the south,\nand you see something shimmer in the distance.\n',56,131072,45,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1151,-30,-33,0,'Overgrown Path','The path is badly overgrown, looking like no living being has treaded it\nin ages.  A little ways to the north, there are some hills, while far\nto the east, you see a volcano.  You seem to be at the beginning of a\nvery dense jungle, which spreads to the south.\n',56,131072,45,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1152,-30,-34,0,'Overgrown Path','The path is badly overgrown, looking like no living being has treaded it\nin ages.  Far to the north, there seems to be some hilly area, while\nfar to the east, the summit of a volcano can be seen.  The path seems\nto lead through a very dense jungle.  Unfortunately the path to the south\nis now completely overgrown and impassable.  The small bit of path that\ndoes exist seems to go right up to a wall of freshly grown vegetation so\nthick it would almost have to be magical.  Maybe with little work someone\ncould open up the path again.\n',56,131072,45,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1155,-1,34,-1,'A Muddy Path','  Apparently newly cut through the thick forest surrounding you this path\nhas yet to become hard packed enough to withstand rain waters.  Deep rutts\nmark the path where wagons must have passed recently.  What appears to be \na campsite of some sorts lies to the west while a large pile of brush is\nbeing used to mask the trail from the main path east of here.\n',16,131076,26,0,0,0,0,-1,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1156,-7,32,-1,'Midair over a Waterfall','Surrounded by a hazy mist of spray from the waterfall below it is hard to\nsee much in any direction and its roar blocks out most of the forest sounds\none would normally hear in this area.  To the northeast is the small lake\nwhich feeds this waterfall while below begins a small forest stream which\ncarries the water away to the southwest.\n',0,131076,34,0,0,0,0,-1,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1160,21,-16,0,'An Overgrown Path','Once a fine road constructed of stone pavers, this smaller path appears to\nhave led from the main road southwest of here to a tower which looms in the \ndistance to the east.  Lack of use or care over the years has allowed the \nplant life and vegetation to grow up between the cracks in the pavers almost\ncompletely concealing their existence.  A large mass of brush and rubble \nlies to the southwest where either the wind or someone seems to have moved it\nabout, concealing the path back to the main road.\n',-1,131076,22,0,0,0,0,-1,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1161,22,-15,0,'An Overgrown Path','  Once a fine road constructed of stone pavers, this smaller path appears to\nhave led from the main road southwest of here to a tower which looms in the \ndistance to the east.  Lack of use or care over the years has allowed the \nplant life and vegetation to grow up between the cracks in the pavers almost\ncompletely concealing their existence.  The trickle of water can be heard\nto the east hinting at a possible fountain or spring.\n',16,131072,22,0,0,0,0,-1,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1162,23,-15,0,'An Overgrown Path','  Once a fine road constructed of stone pavers this smaller path appears \nto have led from the main road west of here to a tower which looms in the \ndistance to the east.  Lack of use or care over the years has allowed the \nplant life and vegetation to grow up between the cracks in the pavers almost\ncompletely concealing their existence.  To the east a huge hole in the \nground marks where a fountain must have once stood, the top of its center-\npiece just barely visible.  A faint mist of water hangs in the air as the \nspring which once fed the fountain pours forth water in various spots, \nnormally in short bursts or streams.  The water quickly collects and trickles \ndown into the hole below where it drains away.  A make-shift path leads to \nthe southeast apparently going around the hole, perhaps used by some occupant\nstill living in the ruined tower to the east.\n',16,131072,22,0,0,0,0,-1,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1163,24,-16,0,'A Make-Shift Path','  Cut through the thick brush and undergrowth this path seems to move about\nthe large hole in the earth just to the north connecting the path to the \neast and west of it.  The earth is hard packed in a thin line where some\noccupant or other of the tower normally walks when traveling this way.  \nSmall streams of water pool and flow north towards the hole which is just \nbarely visible.\n',16,131072,22,0,0,0,0,-1,2,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1164,25,-15,0,'An Overgrown Path Before a Ruined Tower','  Once a fine road constructed of stone pavers this smaller path appears \nto have led from the main road west of here to a tower which looms before \nyou.  Lack of use or care over the years has allowed the plant life and \nvegetation to grow up between the cracks in the pavers almost completely \nconcealing their existence.  To the west a huge hole in the ground marks \nwhere a fountain must have once stood, the top of its centerpiece just barely \nvisible.  A faint mist of water hangs in the air as the spring which once fed \nthe fountain pours forth water in various spots, normally in short bursts or \nstreams.  The water quickly collects and trickles down into the hole below \nwhere it drains away.  A make-shift path leads to the southwest apparently \ngoing around the hole, perhaps used by some occupant of the ruined tower to \nthe east.  A large hole in the base of the tower allows easy access for \nanyone wishing to explore the ruins.\n',16,131072,22,0,0,0,0,-1,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1165,26,-30,0,'A Narrow Rutted Path','The ruts in the path are fairly numerous cutting it in several places \nas the drivers of the wagons tried to avoid previous ruts and the risk of \nbecoming bogged down in the mud that was once present.  Most of the ruts \nare at least twelve inches deep and a couple are almost as deep as two feet.  \nThe path is now dry and hard packed making the ruts quite an obstacle for \none to avoid.  Wagons are most likely bounced about tossing their cargo in \nall directions with the ever present threat to crush a wooden wheel lingering\nover the drivers head.  At the same time any traveler on foot must be wary of \neach foot step or risk turning an ankle.  Vegetation boundaries on both sides\nof the path forcing anyone to remain on it or risk being cut to pieces by the \nvarious thorned underbrush which is all about.  The ruts seemingly end at\na wall of vines to the east but somehow must have passed through.  A path\nleads to the south.\n',-1,131072,24,0,0,0,0,-1,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1166,25,-30,0,'A Narrow Rutted Path','The ruts in the path are fairly numerous cutting it in several places \nas the drivers of the wagons tried to avoid previous ruts and the risk of \nbecoming bogged down in the mud that was once present.  Most of the ruts \nare at least twelve inches deep and a couple are almost as deep as two feet.  \nThe path is now dry and hard packed making the ruts quite an obstacle for \none to avoid.  Wagons are most likely bounced about tossing their cargo in \nall directions with the ever present threat to crush a wooden wheel lingering\nover the drivers head.  At the same time any traveler on foot must be wary of \neach foot step or risk turning an ankle.  Vegetation boundaries on both sides\nof the path forcing anyone to remain on it or risk being cut to pieces by the \nvarious thorned underbrush which is all about.  The path heads north and\nsouth from here.\n',-1,131072,24,0,0,0,0,-1,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1167,24,-30,0,'A Narrow Rutted Path','The ruts in the path are fairly numerous cutting it in several places \nas the drivers of the wagons tried to avoid previous ruts and the risk of \nbecoming bogged down in the mud that was once present.  Most of the ruts \nare at least twelve inches deep and a couple are almost as deep as two feet.  \nThe path is now dry and hard packed making the ruts quite an obstacle for \none to avoid.  Wagons are most likely bounced about tossing their cargo in \nall directions with the ever present threat to crush a wooden wheel lingering\nover the drivers head.  At the same time any traveler on foot must be wary of \neach foot step or risk turning an ankle.  Vegetation boundaries on both sides\nof the path forcing anyone to remain on it or risk being cut to pieces by the \nvarious thorned underbrush which is all about.  The path heads north and\nsouthwest from here.\n',-1,131072,24,0,0,0,0,-1,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
