(1673,-54,-1,0,'Norman\'s Road Through Mordant\'s Deep','  The thick trunks of the trees in this old-growth forest line both sides\nof the road.  The thick undergrowth hinders any movement other than along\nthe road and limits visibility to only a few feet in most directions.  The\nroad itself is made of carefully hewn granite cobblestones fairly well\nmaintained although tree roots are beginning to buckle the paving rocks\nhere and there.  The road continues to the northeast and southwest.\n',19,131072,36,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1674,-55,-2,0,'Southern Entrance to Mordant\'s Deep','  The thick trunks of the trees in this old-growth forest line both sides\nof the road.  The thick undergrowth hinders any movement other than along\nthe road and limits visibility to only a few feet in most directions.  The\nroad itself is made of carefully hewn granite cobblestones fairly well\nmaintained although tree roots are beginning to buckle the paving rocks\nhere and there.  The road continues into the forest to the northeast and\nexits the forest to the south.\n',19,131072,36,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1675,-55,-3,0,'Norman\'s Road South of Mordant\'s Deep','  To the north of you stands a numerous large trees of an old-growth\nforest.  To the south of you, a split rail fence separates you from a\nsection of a vast prairie, which also continues as far as your eyes can see\nto the west.  In the far distance to the east, a range of high mountains\ncan be seen, their tops lost in the clouds.  The carefully hewn granite\ncobblestones which make up the northern part of Norman\'s Road end here at\nthe edge of the prairie, giving way to hard packed dirt.  The road\ncontinues to the north into the forest and across a strange metal grate\nbetween two fence posts to the southeast.\n',19,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1676,-51,11,0,'A Lesser Trail Through Mordant\'s Deep','  The thick trunks of the trees in this old-growth forest line both sides\nof the trail.  The thick undergrowth hinders any movement other than along\nthe road and limits visibility to only a few feet in most directions.  The\ntrail is made of hard packed dirt mixed with leaves and twigs.  The trail\ninterects a larger road to the northwest and disappears into the foliage to\nthe east.\n',19,131072,36,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1677,-54,8,0,'A Lesser Trail Through Mordant\'s Deep','  The thick trunks of the trees in this old-growth forest line both sides\nof the trail.  The thick undergrowth hinders any movement other than along\nthe road and limits visibility to only a few feet in most directions.  The\ntrail is made of hard packed dirt mixed with leaves and twigs.  The trail\ninterects a larger road to the east and disappears into the foliage to\nthe west.\n',19,131072,36,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1683,-48,-10,0,'Norman\'s Road','  Norman\'s Road continues basically north-south here, along the western\nfoothills of the Parnithian Mountains.  To the east, the mountains rise\nmajestically, their tops lost in the clouds.  To the east, a vast prairie\nstretches as far as the eye can see.  The road itself is basically hard\npacked earth, although there are some traces of the gravel that once made\nthis section of the road.  The road continues to the south, and to the\nnorthwest crosses a metal grating set in the road, between two sections of\nfence.\n',19,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1684,-48,-11,0,'Norman\'s Road','  Norman\'s Road continues basically north-south here, along the western\nfoothills of the Parnithian Mountains.  To the east, the mountains rise\nmajestically, their tops lost in the clouds.  To the east, a vast field of\nwheat stretches as far as the eye can see.  The road itself is basically\nhard packed earth, although there are some traces of the gravel that once\nmade this section of the road remaining.  The road continues north and south.\n',19,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(1685,-48,-12,0,'Norman\'s Road','  Norman\'s Road continues basically north-south here, along the western\nfoothills of the Parnithian Mountains.  To the east, the mountains rise\nmajestically, their tops lost in the clouds.  To the east, a vast field of\nwheat stretches as far as the eye can see.  The road itself is basically\nhard packed earth, although there are some traces of the gravel that once\nmade this section of the road remaining.  The road continues north and south.\n',19,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
