(14423,-48,-36,0,'Norman\'s Road','  You are on the southern part of Norman\'s Road.  The road itself is made\nof carefully hewn granite cobblestones meticulously maintained although\nsome weeds and moss grows up between a few of the rocks here and there.\nA wide prairie borders the road to the east, and an open, rolling\ngrassland lies to the west.  The road continues north and south.\n',76,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14424,-48,-35,0,'Norman\'s Road','  You are on the southern part of Norman\'s Road.  The road itself is made\nof carefully hewn granite cobblestones meticulously maintained although\nsome weeds and moss grows up between a few of the rocks here and there.\nA wide prairie borders the road to the east, and an open, rolling\ngrassland lies to the west.  The road continues north and south.\n',76,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14425,-48,-34,0,'Norman\'s Road','  You are on the southern part of Norman\'s Road.  The road itself is made\nof carefully hewn granite cobblestones meticulously maintained although\nsome weeds and moss grows up between a few of the rocks here and there.\nA wide prairie borders the road to the east, and an open, rolling\ngrassland lies to the east.  The road continues north and south.\n',76,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14426,-48,-33,0,'Norman\'s Road','  You are on the southern part of Norman\'s Road.  The road itself is made\nof carefully hewn granite cobblestones meticulously maintained although\nsome weeds and moss grows up between a few of the rocks here and there.\nA wide prairie borders the road to the east, and an open, rolling\ngrassland lies to the east.  In the distance to the northwest, you can\nsee a large white building.  The road continues north and south.\n',76,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14427,-48,-32,0,'Norman\'s Road','  You are on the southern part of Norman\'s Road.  The road itself is made\nof carefully hewn granite cobblestones meticulously maintained although\nsome weeds and moss grows up between a few of the rocks here and there.\nA wide prairie borders the road to the east, and an open, rolling\ngrassland lies to the east.  A large white building sits beside the road\na short ways to the northwest.  The road continues north and south.\n',76,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14428,-48,-31,0,'Norman\'s Road','  You are on the southern part of Norman\'s Road.  The road itself is made\nof carefully hewn granite cobblestones meticulously maintained although\nsome weeds and moss grows up between a few of the rocks here and there.\nA large white building lies to the west, and a wide prairie to the east.\nThere is an intersection to the northwest, and the road continues to the\nsouth.\n',76,131072,22,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14429,-41,-38,-1,'In a Flower Garden','  You find yourself in a small, neatly laid out garden, surrounded by\nflowers of all types.  Paths made of flat stones from the creek wind\ntheir way among the beds, allowing you to view the many wondrous species\nof plants being cultivated here.  Bees and hummingbirds flit between the\nflowers, helping nature by pollinate the plants.  A wrought iron fence\nruns around the northern and western sides of the garden, with a road\npassing by beyond.  There is a passage in the western side.  To the south\nis the wall surrounding the shire, while a small stone house, with a door\nin the middle, sits to the east.\n',76,131076,23,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14430,-40,-38,-1,'Inside a Small House','  Your first thought on entering the main room of this house is that the\nowner is in bad need of a housekeeper.  Dirty dishes, old socks and some\nlarge dust balls are scattered everywhere.  The place has a musty smell\nthat comes from sitting too long with inadequate airflow.  The room\nitself is about twenty feet square, with a six foot ceiling that causes\ntall people to feel like hunchbacks.  A door to the west leads outside,\nand a smaller door to the south leads further into the house.\n',76,131080,32,0,0,0,0,0,8,72,0,'',NULL),
(14431,-40,-39,-1,'A Mage\'s Lab','  This small room can\'t be anything other than the laboratory of a mage.\nA table in the center of the room is filled with beakers and flasks of\nall sizes and shapes, some bubbling fiercely and others giving off\nnoxious vapors.  The back wall is lined with jars containing various\ncreatures suspended in amber fluid.  Stains mar the stone floor and the\nwalls are blacked with soot from experiments gone awry.  A door leads out\nof the room to the north.\n',76,131080,32,0,0,0,0,0,8,96,0,'',NULL),
(14432,-38,-41,0,'Inside a Hobbit Burrow','  You find yourself in the middle of a small sized hobbit burrow, built\ninto the center of a small hillock.  The walls, floor and ceiling are\nmade of wood, if you hadn\'t seen the outside, you would never know you\'re\nin a home below ground and not a free-standing house.  The room itself is\nabout 15 feet square, the six foot high ceiling forces the taller among\nyou to walk slightly bent over.  All the activities of the family take\nplace in this one room.  The front part is the living area, with a eating\narea along the western wall, a kitchen in the back to the north, and\nplaces for sleeping along the wall to the east.  There is a round door in\nthe southern wall.\n',76,131080,32,0,0,0,0,0,8,72,0,'',NULL),
(14433,-38,-43,-1,'In a Pipeweed Patch','  You find yourself in a low, somewhat swampy area along the banks of a\nriver, which lies immediately to your south.  Neat rows of a broadleaf\nplant, which you recognize as pipeweed, have been planted in the wet\nground.  A path runs by the patch to the north, and the patch continues\nto the east.  The wall surrounding the shire lies immediately to the west.\n',76,131072,27,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14434,-37,-43,-1,'In a Pipeweed Patch','  You find yourself in a low, somewhat swampy area along the banks of a\nriver, which lies immediately to your south.  Neat rows of a broadleaf\nplant, which you recognize as pipeweed, have been planted in the wet\nground.  A path runs by the patch to the north, and the patch continues\nto the east and west.\n',76,131072,27,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14435,-36,-43,-1,'In a Pipeweed Patch','  You find yourself in a low, somewhat swampy area along the banks of a\nriver, which lies immediately to your south.  Neat rows of a broadleaf\nplant, which you recognize as pipeweed, have been planted in the wet\nground.  A path runs by the patch along the northern and eastern sides,\nand the patch continues to the west.\n',76,131072,27,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14440,-35,-24,-1,'Headwaters of Whitehawk Creek','  This small pond is the starting point for Whitehawk Creek.  High in the\nParnithian Mountains, it is fed by several underground springs and melting\nsnow, which trickles down the rock cliffs surrounding you.  Reeds and\ngrasses line the shallows, providing food and shelter for the abundant\nwildlife.  The rocky terrain prevents passage any direction but\ndownstream to the southwest.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,60,9,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14441,-36,-25,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 8 feet wide and only about 2 feet deep, but it\nis flowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the banks, providing\nfood and shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom is composed\nof sand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your feet, making\nwalking difficult.  The creek continues upstream to the northeast and\ndownstream to the south.  A small creek branches off to the east.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,60,2,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14442,-35,-25,-1,'Up a Creek','  You are on a small stream that branches off of Whitehawk Creek.  The\nstream flows down out of the rough terrain of the Parnithian Mountains to\nyour east, and intersects with Whitehawk Creek immediately to the west.\nThe only passage you see is downstream to the west.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,90,3,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14443,-36,-26,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 10 feet wide and only about 3 feet deep, but it\nis flowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the banks, providing\nfood and shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom is composed\nof sand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your feet, making\nwalking difficult.  The creek continues upstream to the north and\ndownstream to the south.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,45,2,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14444,-36,-27,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 10 feet wide and only about 3 feet deep, but it\nis flowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the banks, providing\nfood and shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom is composed\nof sand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your feet, making\nwalking difficult.  The creek continues upstream to the north and\ndownstream to the south.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,45,2,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14445,-36,-28,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 12 feet wide and only about 4 feet deep, but it\nis flowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the banks, providing\nfood and shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom is composed\nof sand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your feet, making\nwalking difficult.  The creek continues upstream to the north and\ndownstream to the south.  A small stream branches off to the northeast.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,2,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14446,-35,-27,-1,'On a Small Stream','  You are on a small stream that branches off of Whitehawk Creek.  The\nstream flows down out of the rough terrain of the Parnithian Mountains to\nthe northeast, and intersects with Whitehawk Creek immediately to the\nsouthwest.  The only passage you see is downstream to the southwest.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,90,9,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14447,-36,-29,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 12 feet wide and only about 4 feet deep, but it\nis flowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the banks, providing\nfood and shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom is composed\nof sand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your feet, making\nwalking difficult.  The creek continues upstream to the north and\ndownstream to the south.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,2,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14448,-36,-30,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 12 feet wide and only about 4 feet deep, but it\nis flowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the banks, providing\nfood and shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom is composed\nof sand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your feet, making\nwalking difficult.  A road, which comes out of the mountains, parallels\nthe creek immediately to the east.  The creek continues upstream to the\nnorth and downstream to the southwest.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,9,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14449,-37,-31,-1,'Ford Across Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  The creek\nwidens and slows as it makes a turn here, causing sand to build up.  With\nthe banks on both sides being lower than you have found anywhere else on\nthe creek, this place is often used as a ford.  At this point, the creek\nis about 20 feet wide and only about 3 feet deep.  The creek bottom is\ncomposed of sand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your\nfeet, making walking difficult.  A road, which comes out of the\nmountains, parallels the creek immediately to the east.  A wide prairie\nborders the creek to the west.  The creek continues upstream to the\nnortheast and downstream to the south.  A mud slide has temporarily made\nthe western end of the ford impassable.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,60,2,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14450,-37,-32,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 20 feet wide and about 4 feet deep, but it is\nflowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the banks, providing food\nand shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom is composed of\nsand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your feet, making\nwalking difficult.  A road parallels the creek immediately to the east.\nA wide prairie borders the creek to the west.  A pipeweed patch lies in\nthe lowlands along the river to the south.  The creek continues upstream\nto the north and downstream to the southwest.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,9,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14451,-38,-33,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 20 feet wide and about 4 feet deep, but it is\nflowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the banks, providing food\nand shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom is composed of\nsand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your feet, making\nwalking difficult.  A fence separates the creek from a pasture to the\nsouth.  A wide prairie borders the creek to the north.  A small pipeweed\npatch lies in the lowlands along the river to the east.  The creek\ncontinues upstream to the northeast and downstream to the west.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,3,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14452,-39,-33,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 20 feet wide and about 4 feet deep, but it is\nflowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the banks, providing food\nand shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom is composed of\nsand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your feet, making\nwalking difficult.  A fence separates the creek from a pasture to the\nsouth.  A wide prairie borders the creek to the north and west.  The creek\ncontinues upstream to the east and downstream into a small pond to the\nsouthwest.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,9,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14453,-40,-34,-1,'On the Mill Pond','  You are in the northeast corner of a small pond.  The water is\nmoderately deep, probably no more that 12 feet.  A wide prairie borders\nthe pond to the north, and a pasture sits beyond a fence to the east.\nThe pond continues to the south and west.  A creek flows into this corner\nof the pond from the northeast.  A wooden building can be seen on the far\nbank of the pond to the south.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,75,9,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14454,-41,-34,-1,'On the Mill Pond','  You are in the northwest corner of a small pond.  The water is\nmoderately deep, probably no more that 12 feet.  A wide prairie borders\nthe pond to the north and west.  The pond continues to the south and\neast.  A wooden building can be seen on the far bank of the pond to the\nsoutheast.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,75,2,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14455,-40,-35,-1,'On the Mill Pond','  You are in the southeast corner of a small pond.  The water is\nmoderately deep, probably no more that 12 feet.  A small farm borders the\npond to the east.  The pond continues to the north and west.  To the\nsouth, a loading door leads into a wooden building on the bank of the\npond.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,75,3,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14456,-41,-35,-1,'On the Mill Pond','  You are in the southwest corner of a small pond.  The water is\nmoderately deep, probably no more that 12 feet.  A wide prairie borders\nthe pond to west.  The pond continues to the north and east.  Water flows\nout of the pond to the south, past a water wheel attached to a wooden\nbuilding immediately to the southeast, and over a small earthen dam.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,45,2,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14457,-41,-36,-1,'Atop A Small Earthen Dam','  Water flows across the top of this small earthen dam from the millpond\nto the north.  Footing is treacherous due to the slime growing on the top\nand sides of the dam.  To the east, a water wheel separates the dam from a\nwooden building.  There is a road to the south, and an wide, open prairie\nto the west.  The creek continues to the southwest.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,15,9,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14458,-42,-37,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 30 feet wide and about 5 feet deep, but it is\nflowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the banks, providing food\nand shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom is composed of\nsand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your feet, making\nstanding difficult.  A road parallels the creek to the south and east.  A\nwide prairie borders the creek to the north and west.  There is a dam\nupstream to the northeast and the creek continues downstream to the\nsouthwest.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,9,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14459,-43,-38,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 30 feet wide and about 5 feet deep, but it is\nflowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the northwestern bank,\nproviding food and shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom\nis composed of sand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your\nfeet, making standing difficult.  A road parallels the creek to the east,\nand to the south you see a wall surrounding a small shire.  A wide prairie\nborders the creek to the north and west.  The creek continues upstream to\nthe northeast, and downstream to the southwest.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,9,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14460,-44,-39,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 30 feet wide and about 6 feet deep, but it is\nflowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the northwestern bank,\nproviding food and shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom\nis composed of sand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your\nfeet, making standing difficult.  To the south and east, you see the\nbacks of several buildings in a small shire.  A wide prairie borders the\ncreek to the north, and west.  The creek continues upstream to the\nnortheast, and downstream to the southwest.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,9,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14461,-45,-40,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 30 feet wide and about 6 feet deep, but it is\nflowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the northwestern bank,\nproviding food and shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom\nis composed of sand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your\nfeet, making standing difficult.  To the east, you see the back of an\nimposing brick building.  A wide, open prairie borders the creek to the\nnorth and a field of grain borders the creek to the west.  The creek\ncontinues upstream to the northeast, and downstream to the south.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,2,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14462,-45,-41,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 30 feet wide and about 6 feet deep, but it is\nflowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the western bank,\nproviding food and shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom\nis composed of sand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your\nfeet, making standing difficult.  To the east, you see the back of an\nimposing brick building.  A field of grain borders the creek to the\nwest.  A bridge spans the creek just downstream to the south.  The creek\ncontinues upstream to the north, and downstream to the south.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,2,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14463,-45,-42,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 30 feet wide and about 6 feet deep, but it is\nflowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the western bank,\nproviding food and shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom\nis composed of sand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your\nfeet, making standing difficult.  A bridge spans the creek above your\nhead, if you try, you can probably climb up to it.  The creek continues\nupstream to the north, and downstream to the south.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,2,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14464,-45,-43,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 30 feet wide and about 6 feet deep, but it is\nflowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the western bank,\nproviding food and shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom\nis composed of sand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your\nfeet, making standing difficult.  To the east, you see the back of a\nlarge building.  An open, rolling grassland borders the creek to the\nwest.  Just upstream to the north, a bridge crosses the creek.  The creek\ncontinues upstream to the north, and downstream to the south.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,2,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14465,-45,-44,-1,'On Whitehawk Creek','  You are in the middle of Whitehawk Creek.  This waterway winds through\nthe foothills on the western slope of the Parnithian Mountains.  At this\npoint, the creek is about 30 feet wide and about 6 feet deep, but it is\nflowing fairly swiftly.  Reeds and grasses line the western bank,\nproviding food and shelter for the abundant wildlife.  The creek bottom\nis composed of sand and gravel, which is constantly shifting under your\nfeet, making standing difficult.  To the east, you see a deck off the\nback of a large building.  An open, rolling grassland borders the creek\nto the west.  The creek continues upstream to the north, and downstream\nto the south you can see the intersection where it joins a larger river.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,2,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14468,-32,-45,-1,'Base of Silverstone Falls','  You find yourself in a small pool at the base of the magnificent\nSilverstone Falls.  Mist fills the air, and hearing anything over the\nroaring of the falls is difficult to say the least.  The pool is about 200\nfeet in diameter, and you have no idea how deep.  It has been carved out\nof the rock over the centuries by the action of the water cascading down\nthe falls.  There is a small rock ledge to the north, and the river\ncontinues downstream to the west.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,3,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14469,-33,-45,-1,'On the Silverstone River','  You are in the middle of the Silverstone River.  This broad, swift\nflowing stream runs from the Parnithian Mountains westward to the Agorian\nSea.  The river takes its name from the silvery stones that can be found\nupon its banks.  The river is about 120 feet wide, and over 10 feet\ndeep.  A rock ledge parallels the river to the north, but you can see no\nplace to climb out.  To the south you can see a broad, arid plain.\nUpstream to the east, you can see and hear Silverstone Falls, which\ncascades down from the mountains into a small pool immediately to the\neast.  The river continues downstream to the west.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,3,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14470,-34,-45,-1,'On the Silverstone River','  You are in the middle of the Silverstone River.  This broad, swift\nflowing stream runs from the Parnithian Mountains westward to the Agorian\nSea.  The river takes its name from the silvery stones that can be found\nupon its banks.  The river is about 120 feet wide, and over 10 feet\ndeep.  A path parallels the river to the north, but you can see no place\nto climb out.  To the south you can see a broad, arid plain.  Upstream to\nthe east, you can see and hear Silverstone Falls, which cascades down\nfrom the mountains into a small pool a short ways to the east.  The river\ncontinues downstream to the northwest.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,7,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14471,-35,-44,-1,'On the Silverstone River','  You are in the middle of the Silverstone River.  This broad, swift\nflowing stream runs from the Parnithian Mountains westward to the Agorian\nSea.  The river takes its name from the silvery stones that can be found\nupon its banks.  The river is about 120 feet wide, and over 10 feet\ndeep.  A path parallels the river to the north and east, but you can see\nno place to climb out.  To the south you can see a broad, arid plain.\nUpstream to the southeast, you can hear a loud, roaring sound in the\ndistance.  The river continues downstream to the west.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,3,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14472,-36,-44,-1,'On the Silverstone River','  You are in the middle of the Silverstone River.  This broad, swift\nflowing stream runs from the Parnithian Mountains westward to the Agorian\nSea.  The river takes its name from the silvery stones that can be found\nupon its banks.  The river is about 120 feet wide, and over 10 feet\ndeep.  A path parallels the river in the distance to the north, beyond a\npipeweed patch which lies in the lowlands along the riverbank, but you\ncan see no place to climb out.  To the south you can see a broad, arid\nplain.  Upstream to the east, you can faintly hear a roaring sound.  The\nriver continues downstream to the west.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,3,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14473,-37,-44,-1,'On the Silverstone River','  You are in the middle of the Silverstone River.  This broad, swift\nflowing stream runs from the Parnithian Mountains westward to the Agorian\nSea.  The river takes its name from the silvery stones that can be found\nupon its banks.  The river is about 120 feet wide, and over 10 feet\ndeep.  A path parallels the river in the distance to the north beyond a\npipeweed patch, but you can see no place to climb out.  To the south you\ncan see a broad, arid plain.  Upstream to the east, you can faintly hear\na roaring sound.  The river continues downstream to the west.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,3,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14474,-38,-44,-1,'On the Silverstone River','  You are in the middle of the Silverstone River.  This broad, swift\nflowing stream runs from the Parnithian Mountains westward to the Agorian\nSea.  The river takes its name from the silvery stones that can be found\nupon its banks.  The river is about 120 feet wide, and over 10 feet\ndeep.  A path parallels the river in the distance to the north, beyond a\npipeweed patch, but you can see no place to climb out.  To the south you\ncan see a broad, arid plain.  Upstream to the east, you can faintly hear\na roaring sound.  To the west you can see a small dock at the edge of a\nshire, along the banks of the river.  The river continues downstream to\nthe southwest.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,9,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14475,-39,-45,-1,'On the Silverstone River','  You are in the middle of the Silverstone River.  This broad, swift\nflowing stream runs from the Parnithian Mountains westward to the Agorian\nSea.  The river takes its name from the silvery stones that can be found\nupon its banks.  The river is about 120 feet wide, and over 10 feet\ndeep.  A small dock borders the river to the north, beyond it you can see\nthe backs of several buildings within a shire.  To the south you can see\na broad, arid plain.  The river continues upstream to the northeast, and\ndownstream to the west.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,3,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14476,-40,-45,-1,'On the Silverstone River','  You are in the middle of the Silverstone River.  This broad, swift\nflowing stream runs from the Parnithian Mountains westward to the Agorian\nSea.  The river takes its name from the silvery stones that can be found\nupon its banks.  The river is about 120 feet wide, and over 10 feet\ndeep.  Looking north, you see the back of a wooden building along the\nriverbank.  To the south you can see a broad, arid plain.  The river\ncontinues upstream to the east, and downstream to the west.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,3,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14477,-41,-45,-1,'On the Silverstone River','  You are in the middle of the Silverstone River.  This broad, swift\nflowing stream runs from the Parnithian Mountains westward to the Agorian\nSea.  The river takes its name from the silvery stones that can be found\nupon its banks.  The river is about 120 feet wide, and over 10 feet\ndeep.  Looking north, you see a small hillock, with a chimney rising from\nthe center, along the riverbank.  To the south you can see a broad, arid\nplain.  The river continues upstream to the east, and downstream to the\nwest.  A bridge spans the river downstream to the west.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,3,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14478,-42,-45,-1,'On the Silverstone River','  You are in the middle of the Silverstone River.  This broad, swift\nflowing stream runs from the Parnithian Mountains westward to the Agorian\nSea.  The river takes its name from the silvery stones that can be found\nupon its banks.  The river is about 120 feet wide, and over 10 feet\ndeep.  A wide, stone bridge spans the river over your head, carrying a\nroad from the shire south toward a broad, arid plain.  A set of steps\nleads up the piling on the southern pier to the road above.  The river\ncontinues upstream to the east, and downstream to the west.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,3,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14479,-43,-45,-1,'On the Silverstone River','  You are in the middle of the Silverstone River.  This broad, swift\nflowing stream runs from the Parnithian Mountains westward to the Agorian\nSea.  The river takes its name from the silvery stones that can be found\nupon its banks.  The river is about 120 feet wide, and over 10 feet\ndeep.  Looking north, you see the back of a large wooden building built\nalong the riverbank.  To the south you can see a broad, arid plain.  The\nriver continues upstream to the east, and downstream to the west.  A\nbridge spans the river upstream to the east.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,3,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14480,-44,-45,-1,'On the Silverstone River','  You are in the middle of the Silverstone River.  This broad, swift\nflowing stream runs from the Parnithian Mountains westward to the Agorian\nSea.  The river takes its name from the silvery stones that can be found\nupon its banks.  The river is about 120 feet wide, and over 10 feet\ndeep.  Looking north, you see a large, wooden deck, built onto the back\nof the building along the riverbank.  To the south you can see a broad,\narid plain.  The river continues upstream to the east, and downstream to\nthe west.  Immediately downstream, you can see where this river joins\nwith another stream.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,3,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14481,-45,-45,-1,'River Junction','  You find yourself in the middle of a junction between two major\nstreams.  From the north, Whitehawk Creek flows southward, meeting the\nSilverstone River, which flows in from the east.  The combined stream\nflows out to the southwest.  To the northeast, a small shire sits at the\nriver junction.  A broad, arid plain lies to the south, and an open,\nrolling grassland to the west.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,9,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14482,-46,-46,-1,'On the Silverstone River','  You are in the middle of the Silverstone River.  This broad, swift\nflowing stream runs from the Parnithian Mountains westward to the Agorian\nSea.  The river takes its name from the silvery stones that can be found\nupon its banks.  The river is about 150 feet wide, and over 15 feet\ndeep.  Looking north, you see an open grassland, which extends to the\nhorizon.  To the south you can see a broad, arid plain.  The river \ncontinues upstream to the northeast and downstream to the west.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,3,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(14483,-47,-46,-1,'On the Silverstone River','  You are in the middle of the Silverstone River.  This broad, swift\nflowing stream runs from the Parnithian Mountains westward to the Agorian\nSea.  The river takes its name from the silvery stones that can be found\nupon its banks.  The river is about 150 feet wide, and over 15 feet\ndeep.  Looking north, you see an open grassland, which extends to the\nhorizon.  To the south you can see a broad, arid plain.  The river\ncontinues upstream to the east and downstream to the west.\n',76,131072,29,0,0,0,30,3,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
