(20455,92,-130,2,'Northern Sedmar River','  Making its way from some source far to the northeast the Sedmar River\ncuts through the Chaos Plains slowly slicing deeper and deeper into the\nearth.  While occasional beaches line the shores of the river you find\nit has widened and slowed at this point depositing more fertile soil then\nnormal along this section of the river.  Thick, lush vegetation grows along\nside the waters as tree branches sway overhead creating a partial canopy.\n',113,131072,52,0,0,0,30,9,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(20456,92,-129,2,'Northern Sedmar River','  Making its way from some source far to the northeast the Sedmar River\ncuts through the Chaos Plains slowly slicing deeper and deeper into the\nearth.  While occasional beaches line the shores of the river you find\nit has widened and slowed at this point depositing more fertile soil then\nnormal along this section of the river.  Thick, lush vegetation grows along\nside the waters and tree branches sway overhead creating a partial canopy.\n',113,131072,52,0,0,0,30,2,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(20457,92,-128,2,'Northern Sedmar River','  Making its way from some source far to the northeast the Sedmar River\ncuts through the Chaos Plains slowly slicing deeper and deeper into the\nearth.  While occasional beaches line the shores of the river you find\nit has widened and slowed here depositing more fertile soil then normal\nalong this section of the river.  Thick, lush vegetation grows along side\nthe waters as tree branches sway overhead creating a partial canopy.\n',113,131072,52,0,0,0,30,2,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(20458,93,-127,2,'Overgrown Passage on the Northern Sedmar River','  Stretching out a good distance to either side the river has become\nalmost completely overgrown with vegetation here.  Rich fertile soil\ndeposited by the slow moving waters has provided the perfect habitat\nfor massive quantities of plant and tree life.  Rich with the minerals\nfrom far northeastern mountains the Sedmar River continues its trek to\nthe southwest cutting through the Chaos Plains.\n',113,131072,52,0,0,0,30,9,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(20459,93,-126,2,'A Small Pond Before a Dense Forest','  Stretching out a good distance to either side the river has become\nbasically a pond which is almost completely overgrown with vegetation.\nRich fertile soil deposited by the slow moving waters have provided the\nperfect habitat for massive quantities of plant and tree life.\nUnfortunately it appears the vegetation has become too dense to proceed\nany farther to the north but rich with the minerals from far northeastern\nmountains the Sedmar River continues its trek to the southwest cutting\nthrough the Chaos Plains.\n',113,131072,52,0,0,0,30,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(20460,86,-158,2,'Stagant Pond Below a Grand Natural Dam','  Constructed of large fallen trees, dead vegetation, and dried mud\ndeposited by the river this natural damn has impeded the rivers course\nnorth from its origin from the southern glaciers.  This pond sits at\nthe base of the dam as waters that manage to escape the dams confines\ncontinue on their journey north to Lake Shard.  It appears as though\nthere is no way to the top of the dam, but a screen of vegetation seems\nto be arranged somewhat like a door to the west where a tiny outcropping\nof sand covered in debris and mineral fragments has formed.  The main\npath of the river can be seen to the east.\n',113,131072,27,0,0,0,25,8,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(20461,88,-158,2,'Stagant Pond Below a Grand Natural Dam','  Constructed of large fallen trees, dead vegetation, and dried mud\ndeposited by the river this natural damn has impeded the rivers course\nnorth from its origin from the southern glaciers.  This pond sits at\nthe base of the dam as waters that manage to escape the dams confines\ncontinue on their journey north to Lake Shard.  It appears as though\nthere is no way to the top of the dam while the main path of the river\ncan be seen towards the west.\n',113,131072,27,0,0,0,25,9,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(20462,87,-159,2,'Below the Center of a Grand Natural Dam','  Constructed of large fallen trees, dead vegetation, and dried mud\ndeposited by the river this natural damn has impeded the rivers course\nnorth from its origin from the southern glaciers.  This pond sits at\nthe base of the dam as waters that manage to escape the dams confines\ncontinue on their journey north to Lake Shard.  It appears as though\nthere is no way to the top of the dam at present.\n',113,131072,29,0,0,0,30,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(20463,87,-158,2,'Southern Sedmar River','  Stretching out a good distance to either side the river has become\nalmost completely overgrown with vegetation here.  Rich fertile soil\ndeposited by the slow moving waters has provided the perfect habitat\nfor massive quantities of plant and tree life.  Rich with the minerals\nfrom far souther glaciers the Sedmar River continues its trek to\nthe north cutting through the Chaos Plains.\n',113,131072,29,0,0,0,30,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(20464,87,-157,2,'Southern Sedmar River','  Making its way from glaciers far to the south the Sedmar River cuts\nthrough the Chaos Plains slowly slicing deeper and deeper into the earth.\nWhile occasional beaches line the shores of the river you find it has\nwidened and slowed here depositing more fertile soil then normal along\nthis section of the river.  Thick, lush vegetation grows along side the\nwaters as tree branches sway overhead creating a partial canopy.\n',113,131072,29,0,0,0,30,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
