(25579,-56,-60,-1,'Shifting Dunes Near the Base of the Pyramid','  The desert runs abruptly into the sheer, towering cliffside.  The rock is\nlayered, with shades of red toward the top, about seventy feet from where\nyou stand, then giving way to shades of gray at the bottom.  Just to the\nnortheast stands an enormous pyramid hewn from white marble.  No grooves or\njoints can be detected to suggest that the pyramid was made from many\nblocks of marble, but surely it couldn\'t have been cut entirely from one\nslab.  After all, the structure must be at least 300 feet in height.\n',142,141632,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(25580,-52,-64,-1,'The Atakaji Desert Near the Dunes','  Broad dunes stretch to the south, far too tall to attempt to pass\nthrough.  The desert extends as far as the eye can see.  To the north a\npyramid looms and to the east a town lies.  The dunes are rumored to be the\nhome of some of the worst of all creatures, the sandworms.  What appear to\nbe sunken holes dot the dunes to the south.\n',142,141632,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(25581,-48,-62,-1,'Al Abijah General Store','  Stepping past the curtain, you run almost immediately into a wooden\ncounter.  Extending behind the counter are long shelves so crammed with\ngoods it seems they may collapse.  A short, shrewish looking man appears\nsuddenly from behind one of the shelves and eyes you suspiciously...if it\nweren\'t so obvious that this is the general store, you would think he wants\nyou to leave.\n',142,174585,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25582,-49,-63,-1,'Moamar\'s Meats','  Dried meats of every sort line the walls.  Standing behind the counter,\nMoamar the owner, barely looks up from salting his latest poach of the day,\ndried hobbit.  The only light source in the room trickles in from through\nthe curtain guarding the inside of the shop from the dust and wind outside.\n',142,174585,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25583,-48,-63,-1,'The Desert Outpost of Al Abijah','  With little exception, the buildings in this village are constructed of\nthe same white marble that makes up the surrounding wall.  It\'s easy to\nsense the dual purpose of the material, both reflecting the blazing rays of\nsunlight that beat down and providing firm shelter from howling desert\nsandstorms.  The streets are covered in packed earth and dust.  To the\nsouth sits a small inn, the sides of which are lined with a hitching rail\nto accommodate camels and other mounts used by passing travelers.  A few\nBedouins, their faces covered in light colored cloth, glance suspiciously\nat you as they move swiftly down the street.  Thick, dark curtains cover\nthe entryways of buildings to the west and north.\n',142,141632,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(25584,-47,-63,-1,'Gatehouse of Abijah','  The gatehouse of Abijah was created as an attempt to keep the unwanted\ntravelers out of the town.  With a portcullis on the south and west walls\nof the gatehouse, and a sturdy bronze gate to the northeast the defenses of\nthe town are superb.  Serving as both an entrance and an exit, anything\nthat is to enter or leave the town must pass through here first.\n',142,141632,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(25585,-50,-64,-1,'Magreb\'s Metals and Minerals','  Upon entering this dimly lit shop the smell of ozone hits your nostrils.\nMagreb, dutifully guarding his metallurgy set, is eagerly trying to make\ngold from copper.  Occasionally, an accident occurs and the serious\ninjuries occur.  The sign on the wall is Magreb\'s safety valve.  Written in\nbold letters is \"Enter at own risk\".\n',142,174569,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25586,-49,-64,-1,'The Desert Outpost of Al Abijah','  With little exception, the buildings in this village are constructed of\nthe same white marble that makes up the surrounding wall.  It\'s easy to\nsense the dual purpose of the material, both reflecting the blazing rays of\nsunlight that beat down and providing firm shelter from howling desert\nsandstorms.  The streets are covered in packed earth and dust.  To the east\nsits a small inn, the sides of which are lined with a hitching rail to\naccommodate camels and other mounts used by passing travelers.  A few\nBedouins, their faces covered in light colored cloth, glance suspiciously\nat you as they move swiftly down the street.  Thick, dark curtains cover\nthe entryways of buildings to the west and north.  A large group of people\nis gathered around many tables to the south, bantering back and forth in\nan odd dialect.\n',142,141632,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(25587,-48,-64,-1,'The Oasis Inn','  Stepping into this room, you immediately notice that it seems\nsignificantly cooler in here than it does on the streets of Al Abijah.  The\nsource of this cooler air is not apparent, but wherever it\'s coming from,\nit certainly is refreshing!  A bald, jovial looking man with round glasses\nsits at a large desk in the corner of the room.  Several couches are set up\nfor patrons to lounge on, and a spiral staircase stands in the middle of\nthe room, apparently leading upwards toward the guest rooms.\n',142,174585,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25588,-47,-64,-1,'The Desert Outpost of Al Abijah','  With little exception, the buildings in this village are constructed of\nthe same white marble that makes up the surrounding wall.  There is a dual\npurpose of the material, used for both reflecting the blazing rays of\nsunlight that beat down and providing firm shelter from howling desert\nsandstorms.  The streets are covered in packed earth and dust.  To the west\nsits a small inn, the sides of which are lined with a hitching rail to\naccommodate camels and other mounts used by passing travelers.  A few\nBedouins, their faces covered in light colored cloth, glance suspiciously\nat you as they move swiftly down the street.  Thick, dark curtains cover\nthe entryways of buildings to the east and south.\n',142,141632,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(25589,-46,-64,-1,'The Claw and Fang','  Passing through a heavy, dark curtain, you enter a quiet, dry shop dimly\nlit with a few oil lamps hung from the ceiling.  The walls are filled with\nan array of weapons, ranging from bows and bolos to swords and scimitars.\nBehind the counter stands a tall, grim looking individual.  He wears a\npatch over one eye and is busy sharpening a long, thick dagger.  The\nshopkeeper waves you inside and welcomes you with deep nod.  As he does so\nyou notice the long pink scar on the right side of his face.\n',142,174585,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25590,-49,-65,-1,'Bazaar','  A number of Bedouins are gathered here around several sets of tables,\nhaggling over prices with traders of beads, jewels, rope, cloth,\nfood...just about anything you can imagine could probably be found here.\nThe place is crowded, and the strange clamor of the Bedouin language makes\nit hard to concentrate.  Wooden posts protrude from the walls above the\nbazaar, and a large black drape is stretched across their frame, offering\nthe traders some shade.  Stone rather than packed earth lays beneath your\nfeet, and you notice a small iron grate set into the ground.  To the\nnortheast sits a small inn, the sides of which are lined with a hitching\nrail to accommodate camels and other mounts used by passing travelers.\n',142,141632,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(25591,-48,-65,-1,'The Desert Outpost of Al Abijah','  With little exception, the buildings in this village are constructed of\nthe same white marble that makes up the surrounding wall.  It\'s easy to\nsense the dual purpose of the material, both reflecting the blazing rays of\nsunlight that beat down and providing firm shelter from howling desert\nsandstorms.  The streets are covered in packed earth and dust.  To the\nnorth sits a small inn, the sides of which are lined with a hitching rail\nto accommodate camels and other mounts used by passing travelers.  A few\nBedouins, their faces covered in light colored cloth, glance suspiciously\nat you as they move swiftly down the street.  Thick, dark curtains cover\nthe entryways of buildings to the east and south.  A large group of people\nis gathered around many tables to the west, bantering back and forth in an\nodd dialect.\n',142,141632,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(25592,-47,-65,-1,'The Desert Forge','  Stepping past the curtain, you enter a dimly lit shop.  The surrounding\nwalls are covered with numerous types of armor, some of which is unlike any\nyou\'ve seen before.  A long counter extends along the southeast wall,\nbehind which a portly man is busy polishing a bronze-covered helm.  He\nlooks up from his work and nods to you with a smile.\n',142,174585,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25593,-48,-66,-1,'Rachkir\'s Repairshop','  Standing behind a shabby counter is Rackhir.  Known throughout\nthe land for his shabby repair jobs, Rachkir\'s continued success\nis due only to the fact that he is the only repairman for many\nmiles.  The tools of his trade are lined up neatly on the wall.\n',142,174585,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25594,-49,-65,-2,'Storage Area','  This room was designed as a way for the visiting merchants to store their\nvaluables without having to worry about the thieves about.  It is\nconsiderably cooler than above in the bazaar.  The walls are made of white\nmarble, but the floor is sand.\n',142,141640,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25595,-52,-60,-1,'Inside the Entryway to the Pyramid of Synjilla','  Strange relics dot the walls and hover in the air.  To the northwest this\nhallway opens up to the actual pyramid.  Contained within the pyramid are\nvarious rooms that are used in the process of readying the sacrificed for\ntheir final earthly moments.  Deep within the altar of Synjilla rests,\nready to take the souls of those who are brave enough to submit to the pain\nand anguish that one must go through in order to be sacrificed.\n',142,141640,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25596,-52,-61,-1,'Host of Blood','  Blood stains cover almost all of the marble floors in this room.  Likely\nthe result of the blood letting rack placed neatly in the middle of the\nroom, the blood pools in certain places.  A large vat of blood sits near\nthe southeastern corner.  Rumor has it that Amun Alsiph has a fondness for\nfresh blood and occasionally steps out of his abode to partake of the\ncrimson delight.\n',142,141640,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25597,-51,-60,-1,'Host of Scrolls','  A large bookcase made of wood sits on the east wall.  Filled with various\nscrolls telling of the pain and anguish within are held here as a reference\nfor those who are contemplating going further into the pyramid.  Also\ncontained here are the texts of the history of the Atakaji desert and the\ntrials and tribulations of the people who make this place home.\n',142,141640,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25598,-53,-60,-1,'Host of Flesh','  Pieces of dried flesh line the floor and wall.  A foul stench of rotten\nflesh fills the room and overcomes you.  Placed in the middle of the room\nis the torture rack that is responsible for ripping the flesh from the\nliving.  Flesh giving is one of the stages in the purification process.\nBlood giving and pain are the other two processes that one must go through\nto be eligible for sacrifice.\n',142,141640,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25599,-52,-59,-1,'Host of Pain','  Torture racks of all sorts line the marble walls of this room.  In the\nmiddle of the room lies the most painful of all racks.  Although it may not\nlook like much the rack in the middle of the room is capable of dealing out\nmore pain than most are able to handle.  Naturally, many of the sacrificial\nbeings that are racked here never leave alive.\n',142,141640,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25600,-53,-59,-1,'Fork Of The Damned','  A narrow hallway forks into two directions from here.  To the north and\nwest pits take up most of the ground.  Strange odors fill the room.  Faint\nscreams ebb and flow, barely audible, but definitely noticeable.  Perhaps\nthe rumors of the torture that takes place in this pyramid are true.\n',142,141640,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25601,-54,-59,-1,'Pit of Purification','  This pit is the first of many stages that the sacrificed must go through.\nPart of the ritual involve forcing the subject to roll upon the coals.  The\nwalls bear scars of blood, and the feeble attempts of the entrapped to\nescape.  Racks of some sort of metal are visible on the northern wall.\nUsed to hold those waiting for their turn the racks look sturdy enough to\nhold even the largest of ogres.  Hanging on the rack to the south is a\ndecaying hand of what looks like an elf.\n',142,141632,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(25602,-53,-58,-1,'Pit of Sacrifice','  This is the final part of the first stages of the initiation process for\nthose who will be taken to be sacrificed.  The pit takes nearly half of the\nroom.  The white marble reflects an eerie glow of crimson all around.  The\nsearing heat from the pit can be felt throughout your body.  The hall\ncontinues to the south.\n',142,141640,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25603,-53,-57,-1,'A Narrow Hallway','  This hallway has been left vacant for some time.  The dust and dirt that\nlie across the floor are a testament to how long this hall has been left\nuntouched.  Faint footsteps can be made out that seem to go west.\n',142,141640,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25604,-54,-57,-1,'A Twist in a Narrow Hallway','  This hallway has been left vacant for some time.  The dust and dirt that\nlie across the floor are a testament to how long this hall has been left\nuntouched.  Faint footsteps can be made out that seem to go south.\n',142,141640,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25605,-54,-58,-1,'A Twist in a Narrow Hallway','  This hallway has been left vacant for some time.  The dust and dirt that\nlie across the floor are a testament to how long this hall has been left\nuntouched.  Faint footsteps can be made out that seem to go southwest.\n',142,141640,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25606,-55,-59,-1,'A Twist in a Narrow Hallway','  This hallway has been left vacant for some time.  The dust and dirt that\nlie across the floor are a testament to how long this hall has been left\nuntouched.  Faint footsteps can be made out that seem to go north.\n',142,141640,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25607,-55,-58,-1,'A Narrow Hallway',' This hallway has been left vacant for some time.  The dust and dirt that\nlie across the floor are a testament to how long this hall has been left\nuntouched.  Faint footsteps can be made out that seem to go north.\n',142,141640,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25608,-55,-57,-1,'A Looted Crypt','  Broken vats of sacrificial herbs and incense dot the floor of this white\nmarble room.  Dust and cobwebs fill the room, obscuring your view.  What\nlooks to be bone fragments lie on the floor as well.  Rumors of a ghastly\nentity that still haunt this crypt may be true after all.\n',142,141640,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25609,0,0,0,'A Mist Filled Hallway','  A strange mist fills the room making visibility extremely difficult.  The\nwhite marble walls of this hallway seem to pulse with energy the longer you\nstand in one place.  Echos of pain and laughter fill the air, almost as if\nsomeone was watching you from a concealed place.\n',142,142664,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
(25610,0,0,0,'A Fork in a Mist Filled Hallway','  A strange mist fills the room making visibility extremely difficult.  The\nwhite marble walls of this hallway seem to pulse with energy the longer you\nstand in one place.  Echoes of pain and laughter fill the air, almost as if\nsomeone was watching you from a concealed place.\n',142,142664,40,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,0,'',NULL),
