(27800,43,58,-3,'Bayside Pier','   Solid wooden planks made from teak wood make up the floor boards of\nthis pier.  The large pillars that support this construction descend\ndown deep into the dark blue waters that swirl about under the pier.\nBarnacles and clumps of seaweed cling to the pillars.  Seagulls circle\nthe air high above you, searching for any scraps left over from the days\ncatch.  The water is a good ten to fifteen feet down below you, it would\nbe quite a leap to jump into the cold waters from here.  The shipping docks\nof Amber begin just to the west of here.\n',186,131076,2,40,27800,0,0,-1,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(27801,44,58,-3,'Bayside Pier','Solid wooden planks made from teak wood make up the floor boards of\nthis pier.  The large pillars that support this construction descend\ndown deep into the dark blue waters that swirl about under the pier.\nBarnacles and clumps of seaweed cling to the pillars.  Seagulls circle\nthe air high above you, searching for any scraps left over from the days\ncatch.  The water is a good ten to fifteen feet down below you, it would\nbe quite a leap to jump into the cold waters from here.\n',-1,131072,2,0,0,0,0,-1,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(27802,45,58,-3,'Bayside Pier','   Solid wooden planks made from teak wood make up the floor boards of\nthis pier.  The large pillars that support this construction descend\ndown deep into the dark blue waters that swirl about under the pier.\nBarnacles and clumps of seaweed cling to the pillars.  Seagulls circle\nthe air high above you, searching for any scraps left over from the days\ncatch.  The water is a good ten to fifteen feet down below you, it would\nbe quite a leap to jump into the cold waters from here.\n',186,131072,2,0,0,0,0,-1,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(27803,46,58,-3,'Bayside Pier','   Solid wooden planks made from teak wood make up the floor boards of\nthis pier.  The large pillars that support this construction descend\ndown deep into the dark blue waters that swirl about under the pier.\nBarnacles and clumps of seaweed cling to the pillars.  Seagulls circle\nthe air high above you, searching for any scraps left over from the days\ncatch.  The water is a good ten to fifteen feet down below you, it would\nbe quite a leap to jump into the cold waters from here.  Strangely, no\nseagull droppings seem to be on the eastern pillar.\n',186,131076,2,0,0,0,0,-1,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(27804,47,59,-3,'Bay of Baan\'Clor','   Gentle waves abound as far as your eyes can see, faintly, you think you can\nmake out some sort of wall.  Like lightning striking, insight comes to you!\nYou stand in the renowned bay of Amber.  Providing ships an easy port in any\ntype of weather.  The waters depth is just deep enough so that any ship you\ncan think of can at least drop anchor here.  Most avoid the piers due to the\nruffian type crowd that resides there.\n',186,131072,9,0,0,0,40,2,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
(27805,48,60,-3,'Earthen Retaining Wall','  A deafening roar rattles you to your bones!  Gasping for breath, you ponder\nwhat the sea must be like on the OTHER side of this wall.   Looking at the wall\nitself, you see that its top is ice covered but flat.  The wall is not made\nup of piled up earth as you first thought, but rather granite blocks of\ntitanic proportions are stacked one atop the other.  Small growths of lichen\nand mussels dot the crevices between the blocks\n',186,131072,9,0,0,0,40,9,0,-1,0,'',NULL),
